{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794856407,"nonce":"qhKMWxMqZDXYro16J3xybBoRQtc=","title":"Future.await under Ash: wait on a timed Lock so Ash runs watch handlers","message":"`Future.await` waits with `wait(20)` (a native call) and `Sys.sleep(0.001)`. Under Ash, a library's `ash_host_watch` handlers run only where the runtime polls them: each pass of the main thread's `park` loop (behind `sys.thread.Lock.wait`, `Deque.pop(true)`, `Semaphore.acquire`, `Thread.readMessage(true)`), `host_wait`, and the host's turn. `Sys.sleep` with no Haxe thread alive is a plain thread sleep and polls nothing, and the native wait never returns to the runtime. So an `await` on a future that a watch handler settles never returns; `then` works.\n\nSeen with hxn-gpu-webgpu and hxn-gpu-webgl2 under Ash: `instance.requestAdapter().await()` and `context.completed().await()` hang. Both drivers settle the browser agent's answers through Ash's watch, because a library thread that blocks forever runs Ash's heap out when the page has few thread agents (Ash f6caebf). They document `then` under Ash.\n\nProposed by the Ash session (their issue 7e66212), needing no Ash change: replace the `Sys.sleep(0.001)` in `await` with a timed wait on a `sys.thread.Lock` (`lock.wait(0.001)`) on the main thread, so each slice parks through the runtime and runs due handlers. Applies to the program's main thread only; a worker fiber's park does not run handlers. Ash may also make its no-fiber `sleep_ns` poll watches, or export `ash_host_poll`; either would make the current loop work.\n\nTo verify: the drivers' checks with `#if (emscripten \u0026\u0026 hxn_threads)` widened to all threaded builds, under `hxn check hl --wasm --threads --browser`, including HXN_PAGE_CORES=3.","files":null}]}